{"text": "FDA Receives Record Number of Comments on its Plan to\nRegulate Cigarette Sales and Marketing\n\nPhilip Morris employees, retailers, suppliers and\nconsumers were among the hundreds of thousands of Americans\nwho submitted comments to the Food and Drug Administration\n(FDA), regarding the agency's plan to regulate the marketing\nand sale of cigarettes.\nAccording to Zhe Washington Post, the agency had\nreceived more than 570,000 comments -- an agency record --\neven before the comment period officially ended on January\n2.\n\ns, The Tobacco Institute and other members\n\nPhilip Mo:\n\nbacco industry were among those who\n\nof the nation's\nsubmitted their comments on January 2. The tobacco industry\nheld a press conference, at which it made public its 2,000\npage response to the FDA Commissioner David Kessler's\nunprecedented and illegal plan to sharply regulate tobacco\nproducts. The industry's 2,000 pages of official comments\nwere supplemented by more than 45,000 pages of detailed\nsubstantiation and supporting information.\nAt the industry news conference, Steve Parrish, senior\nvice president, corporate affairs, Philip Morris Companies\nInc., spoke on behalf of the industry. \u201cIt is clear that we\nhave major objections to what Commissioner Kessler has said\nand done. But I want to emphasize with equal weight that\n\nthere is one point on which we all agree:\n\nTIOSITHEOS",
